session:pr738Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #732 was discussed directly on PR #720 (#507): #720 added bgp_has_cyclic_join_graph (a GYO-reduction-based hypergraph acyclicity check) and collect_bgps purely as an internal test assertion confirming the DBLP/W3C suites contain zero cyclic BGPs. Since dagalog's executor only does binary joins with no worst-case-optimal join strategy (#39, parked as exploratory), a genuinely cyclic BGP can hit the O(n^2) intermediate-row ceiling #720 measured, so this issue asked to surface that as a user-facing log::warn! at query-plan time. Read #720's merged code directly (tests/join_reorder_benchmark.rs) rather than relying on the issue text alone, since the issue flagged a real risk: an earlier draft of the detector used pairwise cliques instead of hypergraph/GYO reduction and produced 8 false positives, including flagging a single-pattern query as cyclic -- promoting this to a user-visible warning raises the correctness bar on any false positive. Moved bgp_has_cyclic_join_graph/collect_bgps into a new sparql_parser::cyclic_bgp module, called once from execute_inner per query execution (not per row/solution, matching the issue's explicit cost model), and re-exported both functions #[doc(hidden)] pub so tests/join_reorder_benchmark.rs could import the one real implementation instead of keeping its pre-existing duplicate copy -- removing the exact drift risk the issue's 'Key risk' section warned about. Added the adversarial tests #732 asked for (deeply nested OPTIONAL/GRAPH/UNION, a duplicate pattern, a FILTER-split BGP) as unit tests in the new module, plus an end-to-end execute() test confirming the warning check never changes query results." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:738 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr738Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr738 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr738Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Exposed bgp_has_cyclic_join_graph/collect_bgps as #[doc(hidden)] pub re-exports from sparql_parser's lib.rs, specifically so the separately-compiled tests/join_reorder_benchmark.rs integration test crate could import the one production implementation, rather than leaving two copies of the GYO-reduction algorithm in the codebase." ;
    agp:alternative "Keeping the test file's existing local copy of bgp_has_cyclic_join_graph/collect_bgps untouched (just adding a separate, possibly-duplicated production implementation) was rejected: the issue's own 'Key risk' section explicitly worried about exactly this -- a false positive from an algorithm that silently drifts from its test-validated counterpart over time. #[doc(hidden)] keeps the re-export out of the crate's advertised public API while still letting the one real implementation be reused across the production/test boundary." .
